  "language": "ruby",
  "root": "lib/demo.rb",
  "notes": "Ruby's load path is a convention: `require \"demo/version\"` resolves against a load root that is not stated in the file (the corpus's root is `lib/`), so the corpus is annotated with that root and the extractor must be told it. `require`, `require_relative` and `autoload` are all file-granular: Ruby has no symbol-level import edge at all, so every resolved edge carries symbol `*` and the import relation cannot say *which* name was used. Export is the degenerate case: `module Demo` in five files is one constant, and any public constant or top-level method is nameable from anywhere, so the export set is the public top-level surface and carries almost no information \u2014 which is itself the finding. Truth includes the methods `attr_reader`/`attr_accessor`/`define_method`/`alias`/`defdelegate`/`class_eval`/`Struct.new` create, because they exist for every consumer of the class; whether a *parser* can see them is the question this spike asks, not whether Ruby has them.",
